 
          <p>Not every video game I pick up manages to hold my attention long enough to reach the credits. There are many reasons for this: too boring, too long, not in the right mood, too difficult, too easy, my hardware platform suddenly failed, the friend I played it with stopped playing, &hellip; I realised today that I&rsquo;ve got many of these so-called &ldquo;dangling projects&rdquo;. I&rsquo;m making a list here to help myself get over the initial hurdle of picking them up again. Perhaps I dropped the game for the wrong reasons. Perhaps my mood or the context this time is aligned with that of the game.</p>
<p>This backlog only lists games that are <em>ongoing</em>; meaning that I played at least an hour before turning my attention elsewhere. Shrink wrapped limited editions do not count (to my big relief).</p>
<p><strong>Void Stranger</strong> is a unique sokoban-like game with an emphasis on story and discovery. It&rsquo;s best to go in blind&mdash;and I did&mdash;but the typical Sokoban-like puzzles that stand between me and the unfolding of the story ultimately made me quit playing. I&rsquo;m just not a huge Sokoban fan. I&rsquo;m in B075 and have no idea how deep the Void actually is. According to How Long To Beat, it takes more than 20 hours of puzzling which might be too much for me.</p>
<p><strong>Against the Storm</strong> looked very intriguing because of its unique combination of roguelite mechanics with a typical city builder, yet somehow I stopped playing after one or two storms. I definitely still plan on picking this up, but perhaps after I&rsquo;ve read a newbie guide as the many mechanics can feel daunting for new players.</p>
<p><strong>Wargroove</strong> is a nice <em>Advance Wars</em> clone that gradually revealed its non-story and painfully slow and boring battles. I am more than halfway through the campaign but lost interest after a particularly boring slog of a skirmish that made clear it is definitely not <em>Advance Wars</em>. Perhaps I should push on and do a few more battles before writing up a review. I don&rsquo;t plan to reach the credits for this one: &ldquo;finishing&rdquo; will be playing until I&rsquo;m done. I have this on a physical Switch cart together with <em>Wargroove 2</em> so hope the successor&rsquo;s battles will be more breezy.</p>
<p><strong>UFO 50</strong> is a superb collection of new old games what Joel &amp; I wanted to play by exploring a new game every week. I think we lasted four weeks? That&rsquo;s probably my fault, sorry! I really liked what I played so far but the few stinkers afterwards made me pause. A working strategy would be to explore these games further but after 30 minutes just skip a game if it&rsquo;s not clicking.</p>
<p><strong>Castlevania: Order of Ecclesia</strong> is the last DS <em>Igavania</em> that&rsquo;s noticeably harder than the former two. I already finished it back in the day but wanted to replay them all as part of the <em>Advance Collection</em>. Especially the first few sections are brutal where you&rsquo;ll be barely scraping by. Since I already know where this was going, I lost interest.</p>
<p><strong>Wizardry: Proving Grounds of the Mad Overlord</strong> is a remake of the 1981 original <em>Wizardry</em> &ldquo;blobber&rdquo; RPG. I love <em>Wizardry 8</em> but this game is very obtuse and old school requiring memorisation, grinding, and regularly running back and forth between town and the multi-level dungeon. After a few hours of stumbling around, I found something more interesting to play. I&rsquo;m still not ready to dive back in even though I want to.</p>
<p><strong>Metroid Prime: Remastered</strong> does not need an introduction. I think I played this for just over an hour before putting it down because of&hellip; I can&rsquo;t remember: probably the situation didn&rsquo;t feel right to continue playing. I did play the original back in 2002 but never finished it. Obviously, I do plan to continue exploring these beautiful and strange worlds very soon.</p>
<p><strong>Rayman 2: The Great Escape</strong> is installed on the Win98 Retro PC. I rage-quit a few years ago about halfway through after the 20th attempt to finish one of the annoying race levels. The problem now is that the Win98 PC is dead&hellip; So it&rsquo;ll have to wait until there&rsquo;s time to do some hardware debugging. I don&rsquo;t feel like emulating the console release as I experienced it on the PC and there&rsquo;s quite a difference between the two versions, even when it comes to the levels.</p>
<hr>
<p>For me, the problem with leaving a game way before it&rsquo;s finished is that it creates guilt. This (highly subjective) huge pile is now sitting there, staring at me. <em>What do you mean you&rsquo;re starting a new game? Why not one of us?</em> Good question. Perhaps I should give some more attention to the pile before it completely grows out of control.</p>
<p>It took me a few years to realise that I don&rsquo;t want to spend the little gaming time I have being miserable: I gave up finishing every game I start a long time ago. Yet since I <a href="https://jefklakscodex.com/">write about every game I play</a>, I do feel that I have to put in a significant amount of time before I can form an opinion. That usually means playing all the way to the end, but if that makes me feel miserable, I just won&rsquo;t. I refuse to write about the games that make me feel bad within the first half hour (except weird Game Boy games just for fun) so some just don&rsquo;t make it to the Codex.</p>
<p>Tracking games in progress is not yet difficult. If it is, then I have a problem. The &ldquo;huge pile&rdquo; is not really huge: it&rsquo;s not even a pile yet. I don&rsquo;t track any ongoing nor yet to play games. If you do but don&rsquo;t know where to start, there&rsquo;s stuff like <a href="https://backloggery.com/">https://backloggery.com/</a> or <a href="https://giantbomb.com/game-tracker">https://giantbomb.com/game-tracker</a> or you can make your own like the excellent backlog page <a href="https://blog.gridranger.dev/backlog.html">created by Dávid Bárdos</a>. In a sense, I did make my own, only I publish about the games that are to me considered done.</p>
<p>Although perhaps I should. <a href="https://distantarcade.co.uk/viewing-my-gaming-backlog-as-a-garden/">Distant Arcade wrote about their experience</a> finally carving a spreadsheet to list the tracked ones bring out some harsh truths: <strong>stop buying games and start playing them</strong>! No big surprises there, but still. Getting it to zero, like <a href="https://tylersticka.com/journal/video-game-backlog-zero/">Tyler did</a> might be a bigger challenge than I can ever handle.</p>
